title: Acids, Bases & Reactions
subject: Chemistry
level: GCSE
emoji: ⚗️
description: pH, neutralisation, salts, rates and energy changes.
---
What ion do acids produce in water? :: Hydrogen ions, H⁺.
What ion do alkalis produce in water? :: Hydroxide ions, OH⁻.
Which pH values are acidic, neutral and alkaline? :: Acidic < 7, neutral = 7, alkaline > 7.
Acid + metal → ? :: Salt + hydrogen.
Acid + metal oxide/hydroxide → ? :: Salt + water.
Acid + carbonate → ? :: Salt + water + carbon dioxide.
What salt does hydrochloric acid make? :: A chloride.
What salt does sulfuric acid make? :: A sulfate.
What salt does nitric acid make? :: A nitrate.
What is neutralisation in ionic terms? :: H⁺ + OH⁻ → H₂O
What is the test for hydrogen gas? :: A lit splint makes a squeaky pop.
What is the test for carbon dioxide? :: Bubble through limewater – it turns cloudy.
What is the test for oxygen? :: A glowing splint relights.
What is the test for chlorine? :: Damp blue litmus paper is bleached white.
What is an exothermic reaction? :: Releases energy to the surroundings; temperature rises.
What is an endothermic reaction? :: Takes in energy from the surroundings; temperature falls.
What is a catalyst? :: A substance that speeds up a reaction without being used up.
Name four factors that affect rate of reaction. :: Temperature, concentration/pressure, surface area, and catalysts.
What does a reversible reaction look like in an equation? :: Uses the ⇌ symbol.
What is oxidation in terms of electrons? :: Loss of electrons (OILRIG: Oxidation Is Loss, Reduction Is Gain).
What happens at the cathode during electrolysis? :: Positive ions gain electrons (reduction).
What is the law of conservation of mass? :: No atoms are made or lost in a reaction, so total mass of reactants = total mass of products.
